The cleverest part of the teleprompter isn’t that the speaker repeats the words. It’s that the words can become the speaker’s apparent intention.

That’s the distinction we should preserve in Pantheon: what was supplied, what was reconstructed, what the reader independently contributed, and what was actually authorized.

And the interesting thing isn’t merely detecting those boundaries.

It’s discovering how much original, useful work an intelligence can accomplish while understanding them.

